# Per-call native Merkle membership index

The full unmodified pyparsing preparation reached 3,957 symbols and 55,859 edges in
145.51 seconds, then exceeded the 600-second producer bound during semantic bundle
construction/verification (`results_v5/outcome.json`). Its full bundle root is not
available; no successful whole-repository qualification is inferred from that run.

The XML diagnostic in `profile_diagnostic_v1` profiles the same unmodified native
service. Profiling overhead makes its wall time unsuitable as a normal benchmark.
Of 264.10 profiled seconds, `verify_symbol_merkle_dag` accounts for 179.00 cumulative
seconds. `SymbolMerkleNode.node_cid` is called 242,190 times, accounting for 159.83
cumulative seconds; these times overlap and must not be summed. The verifier
recomputes the complete node-CID set inside each link iteration. The existing DAG
constructor already constructs this same membership set once for its own layering
check.

`merkle_node_index_v1.patch` moves that set construction before the verifier's link
loop. It changes neither content serialization nor CID algorithms, and retains
every block read, canonicality check, CID recomputation, record roundtrip, link
layering check, and index-membership check. It is a local index within one verifier
call, with no global cache, persistent trust, or cached read-path success.

The reviewed native builder constructs `SymbolMerkleDag.symbol_nodes` as a tuple of
concrete `SymbolMerkleNode` values. Both dataclasses are frozen. Node initialization
normalizes incoming/outgoing CID collections and raw-source reasons to tuples;
`node_cid` deterministically hashes an identity payload of those immutable fields.
Thus recomputing that set for every link adds work without changing the result for
these native-built DAGs. The public dataclass's Python annotations do not themselves
prohibit callers from manually supplying mutable collections. This experiment
qualifies native builder outputs and does not authorize concurrent mutation,
hostile subclasses, or arbitrary manually constructed DAG implementations.

The versioned Datasets package uses hardlinks for unchanged package files. The one
changed file is written to a new inode and atomically replaces its variant path;
the original file's hash and inode separation are checked. Bytecode generation is
disabled. The exact source diff, original/variant hashes, and complete runtime
manifest are retained. Normal imports use one selected Datasets package alongside
the complete ASEH Accelerator and Kit, with no duplicate namespace injection.

Required validation is: original native Merkle tamper/layering tests; identical
native XML full-state/bundle roots relative to the completed baseline; cold versus
incremental state/root/reachable-block equality for unchanged and authored source
mutation controls; real capsule freshness/admission and native source expansion;
and the source/objective/policy/environment/byte/current-root guard checks. A changed
toolchain profile intentionally changes a ContextPack's bound toolchain identity,
so an old/new pack CID comparison is not a semantic-root equivalence test.

The bounded profile analyzes the admitted target plus the first four unchanged
package Python paths in fixed registry order. Every other Python file is explicitly
unparsed/raw-required, and all regular-file bytes plus opaque metadata remain
retained. The full profile remains separately attempted. The two scopes must never
be pooled as a single coverage or efficacy denominator.
